```
class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
    this.size = 0;
  }

  push(item) {
    this.items.push(item);
    this.size++;
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Cannot pop from an empty stack");
    }
    this.size--;
    return this.items.pop();
  }

  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      throw new Error("Cannot peek an empty stack");
    }
    return this.items[this.size - 1];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.size === 0;
  }
}
```

This implementation uses the `Array` class to store items and the `size` variable to keep track of the number of items in the stack. The `push` method adds an item to the end of the array and increments the size. The `pop` method checks if the stack is empty and throws an error if it is. If the stack is not empty, it decrements the size and removes and returns the last item in the array. Similarly, the `peek` method checks if the stack is empty and throws an error if it is. If the stack is not empty, it returns the last item in the array. The `isEmpty` method is used to check if the stack is empty.

