To develop a Java application that retrieves data from a SOAP API, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new Java project in your preferred IDE.
2. Add the necessary dependencies to your project's classpath. For example, if you are using Apache CXF, you will need to add the following dependency to your project's pom.xml file:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.cxf</groupId>
    <artifactId>cxf-rt-frontend-jaxws</artifactId>
    <version>3.4.4</version>
</dependency>
```

3. Generate Java classes from the WSDL file of the SOAP API. You can use tools such as Apache CXF's wsdl2java or JAX-WS's wsimport to generate the necessary classes. For example, if you have a WSDL file at http://example.com/api/soap?wsdl, you can generate the Java classes using the following command:

```sh
$ wsdl2java -d src -p com.example.api.soap http://example.com/api/soap?wsdl
```

This will generate Java classes in the src/com/example/api/soap directory.

4. Write Java code to create a SOAP client and call the API. Here's an example:

```java
import com.example.api.soap.*;

import javax.xml.namespace.QName;
import javax.xml.ws.Service;
import java.net.URL;
import java.util.List;

public class SoapClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        URL url = new URL("http://example.com/api/soap?wsdl");
        QName qname = new QName("http://example.com/api/soap", "SoapApiService");
        Service service = Service.create(url, qname);
        SoapApi soapApi = service.getPort(SoapApi.class);

        List<Customer> customers = soapApi.getCustomers();
        for (Customer customer : customers) {
            System.out.println(customer.getId() + ": " + customer.getName());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we first create a URL object that points to the SOAP API's WSDL file. We then create a QName object with the targetNamespace and service name of the API. We use these objects to create a Service object, which we use to get a reference to the SoapApi interface. We can then call the API methods on this interface to retrieve the data.

5. Run the application and verify that it retrieves the expected data from the SOAP API.

